[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-1-1",
    "slot_key": "Ownership of contributed traffic data",
    "slot_value": "Participating agencies contribute and share traffic conditions, incidents, field-device status, planned maintenance or work-zone information, and possibly weather or roadway restriction details if those affect operations; each agency watches its own area, owns its own devices, and is first to monitor local conditions and decide immediate actions inside its jurisdiction. The repository should stay focused on operational data that helps the region coordinate in real time, and agencies should include contact or responsibility information so others know who owns a device or corridor segment when action is needed. For device status records, the repository should capture enough detail to tell whether the issue is an active fault, a degraded condition, or something already expected because of maintenance, including the device location, the type of problem, when it was first observed, and which agency is responsible for that device or corridor. Planned work context should also be visible so other agencies do not treat an intentionally affected device the same way as an unexpected outage. Additional metadata should include the time the problem started, the current status, whether there is a planned work zone, maintenance ticket, or scheduled outage tied to it, and an estimated restoration time if known. Manual entry is allowed for trained operators at the agency responsible for the event or device, and it should capture the same core fields as the automated feed, at least enough to identify what happened, where, when, and who entered it. Usually the agency that first detected or entered the event stays responsible for maintaining it until another agency formally takes it over or provides better verified information.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-1-2",
    "slot_key": "Rules for editing or superseding another agency's data",
    "slot_value": "Non-TMC agencies may acknowledge an alert, add a comment, and submit a request or recommendation, but they must not change regional device settings on their own. Any update they enter should be limited to reporting a local closure, hazard, or lane blockage that affects the shared picture. Anything that could alter device control or published regional information must be blocked unless the agency has been specifically authorized for that asset or situation. Local reports from the field or the agency’s operator enter the shared network as pending or unconfirmed conditions; regional operators review and verify them if needed, then either publish them into the common view or flag them as unverified until more information comes in. Once accepted, the information can update the regional picture and notify other agencies that need to know. If two agencies observe different versions of the same event, the most directly observed and most current report should win, but only after operators compare timestamps, location, and source confidence; if they still cannot reconcile it quickly, both views should remain visible to operators with a note that the event is still being verified rather than forcing a premature correction.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

